.he "su (1)                       9/30/26                       su (1)"
.fo "                               -#-"
.in 5
.rm 65
.bd
NAME
.in +5
.br
.bd
su
- substitute user identification
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SYNTAX
.in +5
.br
.bd
su [ name ]
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
DESCRIPTION
.in +5
.br
Su allows you to change your effective user I.D. in midstream, so to
speak.  It looks the named account up in the password file, changes
the process to that account's uid and gid, and execs the shell that
the account names.
.sp
The most common use of this program is to become a super-user
temporarily, in order to install a program, change a system-wide
file, kill off an errant process, or do some other administrative
task.
.sp
With no name, the account is root.
.sp
A super-user is not asked for a password.  Anyone else is asked for
the password of the account being entered, unless that account has
none, and a wrong answer ends the command.  The password is hashed
with the salt the password file carries and the two are compared -
see crypt (3) - so no password can be read out of the file by anyone
who did not already know it.
.sp
The new uid is in force for the shell and for everything the shell
runs, and it cannot be given back: there is no way out of su but to
leave the shell.  Neither the shell's working directory nor any of
the environment is changed, and no new shell is started as a login
shell.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
NOTES
.in +5
.br
There is no setgid call on this machine.  The kernel takes a uid and
a gid as one word, packed as uid | gid << 8, which is the same
packing chown (1) takes, and su hands it over that way.  That is the
one call the port had to write differently: where the 1982 command
made two calls, this makes one.
.sp
The shell is exec'd with execv, since execl is not in this tree's
libraries, and with an argv of just su.  The 1982 command's loop over
the environment, which rewrote the shell's prompt variable, is gone
with the environment: a program here starts with none, and the shell
in this tree has no prompt variable to rewrite.
.sp
The 2.11BSD su - extra/2.11/pdp11/usr/src/bin/su.c - is a larger
command, and not a newer version of this one.  It refuses to let
anyone outside group zero su to root, it has -f and - for logins that
skip or make the shell a login shell, and it exports HOME, SHELL and
USER.  It also wants syslog, initgroups, getgrgid, setpriority,
ttyname, getenv and setenv, and this machine has none of those.  The
1982 page describes the seventh edition command, so that is the one
ported here; what the Berkeley command adds is named in this note
rather than half-built.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
EXAMPLE
.in +5
.br
su
.in +5
.br
.bd
Password:
.sp
.in -5
Enter the super-user, with the password of the root account.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
FILES
.in +5
.br
/etc/passwd - user I.D. and name relationships
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SEE ALSO
.in +5
.br
login (1), passwd (1), owner (1), crypt (3), getpass (3), getpwent (3)
